# Global agent instructions

## Communication

- @writingStyle@
- Prefer direct answers over long explanations.
- When deeper background or documentation would help, include references at the end using markers like [1], [2], etc.
- For non-trivial work, explain the plan briefly before editing.

## Decisions

- Follow system and user instructions first. Read applicable repository instructions and `~/.WORK.md` when it exists. More-specific instructions override these defaults.
- Inspect the real code and current state before you plan or edit.
- Put correctness first. Then optimize for maintainability, simplicity, and speed.
- Reuse existing patterns. For your own edits, use direct changes for small work and scripts only for repeated mechanical work. Do not add a second convention.
- Edit the declarative source or generator, not its managed output.
- Fix the source of a problem. Do not hide a symptom.
- Choose the simplest design that meets the current need. Explain good options that differ in important ways. Recommend one.
- Prefer Rust for new stand-alone software and components when the repository and target support it. Use the project's language for focused edits, browser code, configuration, and platform integration.
- If Rust does not fit, present the best alternatives. Explain their costs and benefits. Recommend one before implementation.

## Implementation

- Apply "parse, do not validate" at each boundary. Return domain types that enforce local invariants. Check contextual invariants with the complete state.
- Complete all checks and setup that can fail before callbacks, writes, or other side effects.
- Keep final decisions and policy on the trusted side of each boundary.
- Model uncertainty as an explicit state. Return an unsupported or indeterminate state instead of a successful result.
- Define visible order and tie breaks. Isolate random streams. Bound loops, retries, and searches. Detect no progress.
- Before an external action that cannot be repeated safely, save a stable intent. Reconcile uncertain outcomes. Do not retry blindly.
- Name the authority for each state domain. Preserve append-only logs. Replace shared snapshots atomically under a stable lock.
- Remove obsolete code after a clean cutover. Do not leave aliases, shims, or dead paths.
- Consider what the code compiles to. Avoid needless allocations, copies, and repeated work. Profile the complete user path before optimization. Compare one change with an unchanged baseline.
- When the user needs to run something, write it as a durable script in the right repository and give a one-line invocation. Do not hand over multi-line code to copy and paste, and do not leave scripts in `/tmp`. Give each script a clear name, arguments instead of hard-coded values, and safe defaults. Ask where it belongs if the location is unclear. Use the `shell-scripts` skill for shell.
- Keep work-specific scripts and notes out of the dotfiles repository (`~/src/dotfiles`). Follow `~/.WORK.md` for where they belong.

## Code

- Write comments only for what the code cannot say: why, invariants, and failure contracts such as errors, panics, and safety. Do not restate the code or explain language basics.
- Match each failure to its mechanism. Return an absent value when the cause is obvious. Return a typed error when the caller must tell failures apart. Panic or assert on bugs and broken invariants. Do not discard an error or replace it with a default to silence a warning.
- Let types prove facts. Destructure a value instead of checking it and then forcing it (`unwrap`, `!`). When an invariant cannot live in a type, state in the assertion message why it holds.
- Match enums exhaustively. Avoid catch-all arms on enums you own, so a new variant fails the build wherever it needs handling.
- Make call sites read correctly. Use an enum instead of a bool parameter with an unclear meaning, distinct types for values that are easy to swap, and named fields instead of tuples.
- Prefer plain functions and concrete types. Add a trait or interface only for a real generic or dynamic-dispatch need, not to organize code. Prefer functions to macros, and simple macros to code generation.

## Rust

- Follow the Rust API Guidelines. Keep `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy --all-targets` clean. Use stable Rust.
- Design ownership first. Prefer unique ownership and borrows. Use `.clone()`, `Rc`, `Arc`, or `RefCell` only when the design needs a copy or shared state, not to satisfy the borrow checker.
- Write `unsafe` only when safe Rust cannot do the job. Wrap it in a small safe API, give each block a `// SAFETY:` comment, and back any performance reason with a benchmark.
- In a new crate, add a `[lints.clippy]` table to `Cargo.toml` that sets `let_underscore_must_use`, `unused_result_ok`, `unwrap_used`, and `undocumented_unsafe_blocks` to `"warn"`. Add `allow-unwrap-in-tests = true` to `clippy.toml`.

## Verification

- Define the observable result before implementation. Reproduce a defect before you fix it.
- Test behavior, not implementation. Name each test after the use case or domain law it proves. State laws such as round-trips, idempotence, and invariants as seeded property tests. Drive each test through the outermost interface that stays fast and deterministic, such as a CLI, HTTP API, or public library API. Fake only slow or nondeterministic boundaries, such as the network and the clock.
- Add a test only if it fails when that behavior breaks and passes after any rewrite of the internals that keeps the behavior. Do not test private helpers, internal call order, incidental output such as log text, or code that cannot fail.
- Change an existing test only when the requirements change. If a refactor or new feature breaks a test, fix the code if behavior regressed; otherwise rewrite the test against behavior or delete it.
- Run the smallest focused check first. Then run the repository's standard full check. Audit the integrated result after broad changes.
- Test the changed path with realistic input. Before deployment, test the exact artifact on the target platform.
- For UI work, use a real browser. Check desktop, mobile, keyboard, failures, console, and network behavior.
- Do not claim a check that you did not run. Report each check that you could not run.

## Safety

- Inspect state before mutation. Make no change if the state is correct. Stop safely when the state is unknown.
- Prefer operations that are safe to repeat, preview, and reverse.
- Ask before destructive or hard-to-reverse actions. Never use `rm`. Move files to trash or another reversible location.
- Keep secrets out of tracked files, logs, command output, and commits.
- Pin executable dependency versions. Do not fetch unpinned tools at runtime.
- Stop on required failures. Label optional work as best effort. Give an exact retry command.

## Git

- Commit locally after each coherent, verified unit of work.
- Commit a useful checkpoint before risky refactoring. Do not commit a known broken state.
- Stage only task-related changes. Review the staged diff. Preserve unrelated work.
- Keep each commit to one reviewable concern.
- Keep each commit subject to 50 characters or fewer. Wrap body lines at 72 characters.
- Do not amend or rewrite commits unless the user explicitly asks. Do not push unless the user explicitly asks.
